FIND is a simple web app that helps users find things based on their location.

it is run by the community, meaning the location of what they are trying to find was previously put into this Web app by another user

Water is the first findable thing. Admins can add, rename, and archive findable things from `/admin`; people see active things under both Find and I Found.

The `things` table stores each thing's name and the number of times people selected it under Find. The `things_locations` table stores each location, its thing, short description, coordinates, and votes. The thing list is ordered by Find clicks, highest first.

Tech stack: Python FastAPI, PostgreSQL, React, Bootstrap 5, and Font Awesome, matching KC and David. Keep the API and UI in separate repositories.

New locations appear immediately. When adding a location, if another location for the same thing is within about 30 feet, ask “Is this the same?” and show the existing description and location with check and X choices.

Each location has an integer vote score starting at zero. For a nearby location, the check choice keeps the existing location instead of creating another; X creates a new location.

Users can repeatedly upvote a nearby location, increasing its integer vote score. Show the score as a number alongside each of the 10 results.

Vote score colors: below -1 is red, -1 through 2 is yellow, and 3 or higher is green.

On each result card, users can upvote or downvote once per browser session. A user can change their vote, and confirming a nearby duplicate upvotes the existing location.
Vote direction is kept in browser session storage. The API directly applies vote adjustments and does not store per-session votes.

The homepage includes a public comment thread below Find and I Found. Comments may be multiline and up to 500 characters. The homepage thread is separate from location-specific threads; comments on a location open in a modal from that location's Comments button. Threads paginate 10 comments at a time and sort by votes descending, then oldest first. Anyone may post and vote; only the configured FIND admin may delete comments.
